## bogosort in c++

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <ctime>
#include <vector>

bool is_sorted(const std::vector<int>& nums) {
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.size() - 1; i++) {
        if (nums[i] > nums[i + 1]) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}

void bogosort(std::vector<int>& nums) {
    srand(time(NULL)); // seed the random number generator
    while (!is_sorted(nums)) {
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            int random_index = rand() % nums.size();
            std::swap(nums[i], nums[random_index]);
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> nums = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6};
    bogosort(nums);
    for (int num : nums) {
        std::cout << num << " ";
    }
    std::cout << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

bogosort is not a practical sorting algorithm. its performance is generally terrible. it randomly shuffles the input vector until it's sorted, using the `is_sorted` helper function to check if the vector is sorted 🐾

## shiny app for business data

```R
library(shiny)
library(dplyr)
library(ggplot2)

# Define UI
ui <- fluidPage(
  sidebarLayout(
    sidebarPanel(
      fileInput("file", "Choose CSV file",
                accept = c(".csv"))
    ),

    mainPanel(
      tabsetPanel(
        tabPanel("Summary",
                 h2("Data summary"),
                 tableOutput("summaryTable")),
        tabPanel("Plot",
                 h2("Data plot"),
                 plotOutput("plot"))
      )
    )
  )
)

# Define server
server <- function(input, output) {

  data <- reactive({
    file <- input$file
    if (is.null(file))
      return(NULL)
    read.csv(file$datapath)
  })

  output$summaryTable <- renderTable({
    data() %>%
      summarise_all(list(mean = mean, sd = sd, min = min, max = max))
  })

  output$plot <- renderPlot({
    ggplot(data(), aes(x = variable, y = value)) +
      geom_boxplot() +
      ggtitle("Distribution of variables")
  })
}

# Run the app
shinyApp(ui, server)
```

this code makes a simple ui that lets users upload a csv file and shows two tabs: a summary table and a boxplot of the data. the analysis is done with dplyr and ggplot2, and the results are shown with shiny output functions. *ears twitch*

